<BaseLayout title="About Dana — Product Designer" description="About Dana Forough Dabagh, a product designer focused on healthcare, insurance, accessibility, and complex digital services.">
  <header class="about-hero"><div class="container hero-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">About me</p><h1>I find the structure inside complicated problems.</h1><p class="lead">I’m Dana Forough Dabagh, a product designer with 5+ years of experience across healthcare, insurance, and enterprise products. I turn complex requirements and fragmented systems into experiences people can understand and use with confidence.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/Forough-Dabagh-Resume.pdf" download>Download résumé</a><a class="button button--secondary" href="#contact">Contact me</a></div></div><aside class="principle"><p>My approach</p><blockquote>Understand the system deeply, then decide how much of that complexity the user actually needs to experience.</blockquote></aside></div></header>

  <section><div class="container story"><div><p class="eyebrow">My story</p><h2>From curiosity about systems to designing them</h2></div><div><p>I have always been drawn to the “why” behind things: how parts connect, where a process breaks down, and what makes information easier to understand. That curiosity eventually became a career in UX and product design.</p><p>At Canada Life, I have worked on responsive group-benefits experiences within a regulated environment, collaborating with product, business analysis, research, content, engineering, QA, and accessibility partners. My work has included hundreds of Jira tickets and major redesigns spanning medical claims, estimates, account information, and supporting workflows.</p><p>Earlier, at Dialogue, I designed healthcare and B2B experiences and conducted research into the tools employees used to manage customer accounts. Across both organizations, my role has often been to make a complicated service feel coherent without ignoring the business and technical systems behind it.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2>Evidence first, clarity throughout</h2></div><div class="values"><article><span>01</span><h3>Find the real problem</h3><p>I use interviews, feedback, analytics, workflow mapping, and existing-product evidence to understand what is actually creating friction.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Design the system, not only the screen</h3><p>Information architecture, terminology, entry points, and handoffs can matter as much as the interface itself.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Stay involved through delivery</h3><p>I collaborate with engineering, accessibility, content, and QA so the intended experience survives implementation.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Current direction</p><h2>Connecting product design, accessibility, and emerging technology</h2></div><div><p>I’m an admitted graduate student in the Master of Liberal Arts (ALM) program in Digital Media Design at Harvard Extension School. My studies include web application development, UX engineering, interactive prototyping, and creative AI.</p><p>I’m especially interested in how AI can improve accessibility and reduce the cost of making complex information usable. That interest is informing current work on tools for transforming inaccessible PDF forms into more accessible, code-based experiences.</p><p>My front-end knowledge also helps me prototype ideas, communicate with developers, and evaluate how design decisions behave beyond the Figma file.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="experience"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Experience</p><h2>Designing across health tech and insurance</h2></div><div class="timeline"><article><div><strong>Canada Life</strong><span>2022–present</span></div><h3>User Experience Designer</h3><p>Responsive group-benefits experiences, research synthesis, enterprise workflows, platform migration, accessibility, and design-to-development collaboration.</p></article><article><div><strong>Dialogue Technologies</strong><span>2022</span></div><h3>Product Designer</h3><p>Healthcare and B2B dashboards, employee research, account-management discovery, onboarding, and agile product delivery.</p></article><article><div><strong>Humber Galleries</strong><span>2021</span></div><h3>UX Designer Intern</h3><p>An immersive digital storytelling experience created for Nuit Blanche Toronto.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="education"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Education and learning</p><h2>A multidisciplinary foundation</h2></div><ul><li><strong>Harvard Extension School</strong><span>Admitted graduate student, Master of Liberal Arts (ALM) in Digital Media Design</span></li><li><strong>Nielsen Norman Group</strong><span>Interaction Design specialization</span></li><li><strong>Humber Polytechnic</strong><span>Post-Graduate Certificate, User Experience Design</span></li><li><strong>University of Tehran</strong><span>B.A. in Foreign Languages, with post-graduate business studies</span></li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="contact" id="contact"><div class="container contact-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">Contact</p><h2>Let’s talk about thoughtful, useful products.</h2><p>I’m open to conversations about product-design roles, accessibility, healthcare technology, and collaborative projects.</p></div><div class="contact-actions"><a class="button" href="mailto:forough.dabagh0@gmail.com">Email me</a><a class="button button--light" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/dana-forough-dabagh-71885880/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a></div></div></section>

  <style>
    .about-hero{padding:clamp(5rem,11vw,9rem) 0;background:radial-gradient(circle at 88% 18%,rgba(36,125,134,.16),transparent 25rem),linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#f7fbfb)}.hero-grid,.grid,.story{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.05fr .95fr;align-items:center;gap:clamp(3rem,8vw,8rem)}.lead{max-width:46rem;font-size:clamp(1.2rem,2.4vw,1.55rem)}.principle{padding:clamp(1.8rem,4vw,3rem);border:1px solid rgba(36,125,134,.25);border-radius:1.4rem;background:#fff;box-shadow:0 1.25rem 4rem rgba(21,36,59,.08)}.principle>p{color:#247d86;font-size:.8rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}.principle blockquote{margin:1.5rem 0 0;color:var(--color-ink);font-size:clamp(1.35rem,3vw,2rem);font-weight:700;line-height:1.3}section{padding:clamp(5rem,10vw,9rem) 0}.story{align-items:start}.story>div:last-child p:first-child{margin-top:0}.tinted,.education{background:var(--color-surface)}.section-head{max-width:52rem;margin-bottom:3rem}.values{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:1.25rem}.values article{padding:2rem;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:1rem;background:#fff}.values span{color:#247d86;font-weight:800}.values h3{margin:2rem 0 .8rem}.experience{background:#fff}.timeline{border-top:1px solid var(--color-border)}.timeline article{display:grid;grid-template-columns:.8fr 1fr 1.6fr;padding:2rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-border);gap:2rem}.timeline article div{display:flex;flex-direction:column}.timeline strong,.education strong{color:var(--color-ink)}.timeline span,.education span{color:var(--color-muted)}.timeline h3,.timeline p{margin:0}.education ul{margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none}.education li{display:grid;padding:1.2rem 0;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);gap:.3rem}.contact{color:#fff;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#10213a,#2458c6)}.contact h2{max-width:16ch;color:#fff}.contact .eyebrow{color:#afc9ff}.contact p{max-width:40rem}.contact-inner{display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:space-between;gap:3rem}.contact-actions{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.75rem}.button--light{border-color:#fff;color:#fff;background:transparent}@media(max-width:52rem){.hero-grid,.grid,.story,.values{grid-template-columns:1fr}.timeline article{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.5rem}.contact-inner{align-items:flex-start;flex-direction:column}}
  </style>
